<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermopolis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khemenu, el-Ashmunein, Hermopolis Magna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermopolis, ancient Egyptian Khemenu and modern el-Ashmunein, was a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> and the chief cult centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, god of wisdom and writing. Its priests taught a creation myth of eight primordial gods, the Ogdoad. In the 8th century BCE it was the seat of King Nimlot, whom the Kushite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> besieged, and in Roman times it was a large provincial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermopolis.jpg" alt="Hermopolis" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AshmuneinBasilica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
